---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import auditEvents from "../../../data/generated/audit-events.json";

const eventTypes = auditEvents as string[];
---

<DocsLayout title="Audit Logging">
  <h1>Audit Logging</h1>
  <p>
    Identity writes an <code>AuditEvent</code> row to the datastore for every
    security-relevant action. The <code>pkg/audit</code> logger is
    append-only — events are never updated or deleted. Admins query the
    log via <code>ListAuditEvents</code>.
  </p>

  <aside class="not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/40 p-4 text-sm">
    <strong>The event-type list is generated from source.</strong> The
    canonical identifiers below are extracted from the <code>EventType</code>
    constants in <code>pkg/audit/logger.go</code> by <code>cmd/docsgen</code>,
    and a CI drift guard fails the build if these docs reference an event that
    no longer exists. To add or rename an event, edit the Go source (not this
    page), then regenerate with <code>make docs-gen</code>.
  </aside>

  <h2>What gets logged</h2>
  <p>By area:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Login attempts: success, failure, login-while-locked, lockout opened (threshold tripped), OAuth, passkey.</li>
    <li>Session: logout, session revoke.</li>
    <li>Password lifecycle: change, reset, admin reset-password.</li>
    <li>Passkey lifecycle: add, remove.</li>
    <li>TOTP lifecycle: enable, disable, verify.</li>
    <li>Phone verification: requested, verified.</li>
    <li>QR login: approved, rejected.</li>
    <li>Linked identities: linked, unlinked.</li>
    <li>User-management actions: invite, deactivate, reactivate, delete.</li>
    <li>Admin help requests: requested, resolved.</li>
    <li>Control-plane changes: login-policy upsert/delete, project-config update, platform-admin bootstrap blocked.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Event types</h2>
  <p>
    The complete set of <code>event_type</code> values you can filter
    <code>ListAuditEvents</code> by:
  </p>
  <ul class="columns-2 sm:columns-3">
    {eventTypes.map((ev) => (<li><code>{ev}</code></li>))}
  </ul>

  <h2>Event shape</h2>
  <Code code={`message AuditEvent {
  string id              = 1;
  string event_type      = 2;  // see AuditEventType enum
  string actor_user_id   = 3;
  string target_user_id  = 4;
  string ip_address      = 5;
  string user_agent      = 6;
  bool   success         = 7;
  string details         = 8;  // JSON blob, event-specific
  Timestamp created_at   = 9;
}`} lang="proto" />

  <h2>Querying</h2>
  <p>
    <code>ListAuditEvents</code> is admin-only and supports filtering by
    actor, target, event type, and a time range, plus cursor pagination.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/ListAuditEvents \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $ADMIN_TOKEN" \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{
    "eventType": "login_failure",
    "startTimeMs": 1735689600000,
    "limit": 100
  }'`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>Retention</h2>
  <p>
    Identity does not delete audit events. Retention is the operator's
    responsibility — typically a periodic job that prunes events
    older than the compliance window. Because the events are plain
    relational rows, a simple time-bucketed <code>DELETE</code> is
    sufficient.
  </p>

  <h2>Forwarding</h2>
  <p>
    The audit logger writes to the datastore and to the standard structured zap
    logger. To forward to a SIEM, scrape the container's stdout with your
    log collector — every event also surfaces as a structured log line
    tagged <code>audit_event</code>.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
